.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt MDOC 7
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm mdoc
.Nd semantic markup language for manual pages
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
language describes a manual page by what its parts mean \(em a flag, an
argument, a file name, a cross-reference \(em rather than by how they look.
It is written as
.Xr roff 7
input: text lines, and macro lines starting with a dot and a macro name.
OxideBSD's own manual pages are written in it, and
.Xr oxdoc 1
formats it.
.Pp
A macro's arguments are separated by spaces; an argument containing spaces
is quoted with double quotes, and a double quote inside one is written
twice.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
\&.Nm
utility reads
\&.Ar file
and writes
\&.Qq results .
.Ed
.Pp
Text lines are filled and adjusted into paragraphs.
A sentence should end at the end of an input line, so that it can be
followed by two spaces; a new sentence starts on a new line.
Blank lines should not be used; paragraphs are separated with
.Ic \&Pp .
.Sh MANUAL STRUCTURE
A page starts with a prologue of three macros and continues with sections,
in this order where present:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.Dd Month day, year
\&.Dt TITLE section [architecture]
\&.Os [system [version]]
\&.Sh NAME
\&.Nm name , name ...
\&.Nd one-line description
\&.Sh LIBRARY            (sections 2, 3, 9)
\&.Sh SYNOPSIS
\&.Sh DESCRIPTION
\&.Sh CONTEXT            (section 9)
\&.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
\&.Sh RETURN VALUES      (sections 2, 3, 9)
\&.Sh ENVIRONMENT
\&.Sh FILES
\&.Sh EXIT STATUS        (sections 1, 6, 8)
\&.Sh EXAMPLES
\&.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
\&.Sh ERRORS             (sections 2, 3, 4, 9)
\&.Sh SEE ALSO
\&.Sh STANDARDS
\&.Sh HISTORY
\&.Sh AUTHORS
\&.Sh CAVEATS
\&.Sh BUGS
\&.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.Ed
.Pp
The sections are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 1
General commands.
.It 2
System calls.
.It 3
Library functions.
.It 4
Device drivers.
.It 5
File formats.
.It 6
Games.
.It 7
Miscellaneous information.
.It 8
System administration.
.It 9
Kernel interfaces.
.El
.Sh MACRO OVERVIEW
.Ss Document structure
.Bl -column "Brq, Bro, Brc" description -compact
.It Ic \&Dd Ta date: Ar month day , year
.It Ic \&Dt Ta title: Ar TITLE section Op Ar arch
.It Ic \&Os Ta operating system: Op Ar system Op Ar version
.It Ic \&Sh Ta section heading
.It Ic \&Ss Ta subsection heading
.It Ic \&Sx Ta reference to a section or subsection
.It Ic \&Pp Ta paragraph break
.It Ic \&Nm Ta the name of the page's subject
.It Ic \&Nd Ta one-line description, in NAME
.El
.Ss Displays and lists
.Bl -column "Brq, Bro, Brc" description -compact
.It Ic \&Bd , \&Ed Ta display block:
.Fl literal ,
.Fl filled ,
.Fl ragged ,
.Fl unfilled ,
.Fl centered ;
.Fl offset Ar width ;
.Fl compact
.It Ic \&D1 Ta indented display of one line
.It Ic \&Dl Ta indented literal display of one line
.It Ic \&Bl , \&El Ta list block:
.Fl tag ,
.Fl hang ,
.Fl ohang ,
.Fl inset ,
.Fl diag ,
.Fl bullet ,
.Fl dash ,
.Fl enum ,
.Fl item ,
.Fl column ;
.Fl width Ar width ;
.Fl offset Ar width ;
.Fl compact
.It Ic \&It Ta list item
.It Ic \&Ta Ta column separator in a column list
.It Ic \&Bk , \&Ek Ta keep the words of each line together
.It Ic \&Bf , \&Ef Ta font block:
.Fl emphasis ,
.Fl literal ,
.Fl symbolic
.El
.Ss Commands and options
.Bl -column "Brq, Bro, Brc" description -compact
.It Ic \&Fl Ta a command-line flag; each argument gets a dash
.It Ic \&Ar Ta a command-line argument; default
.Ar file ...
.It Ic \&Cm Ta a command modifier or keyword
.It Ic \&Ic Ta an internal or interactive command
.It Ic \&Op Ta an optional part, in brackets
.It Ic \&Oo , \&Oc Ta an optional part spanning lines
.It Ic \&Ev Ta an environment variable
.It Ic \&Pa Ta a file or directory name
.It Ic \&Ex Ta the standard exit status text:
.Fl std Op Ar utility ...
.El
.Ss Programming interfaces
.Bl -column "Brq, Bro, Brc" description -compact
.It Ic \&Lb Ta a function library
.It Ic \&In Ta an include file
.It Ic \&Fd Ta a preprocessor directive
.It Ic \&Ft Ta a function's type
.It Ic \&Fn Ta a function:
.Ar name Op Ar argument ...
.It Ic \&Fo , \&Fc Ta a function over several lines
.It Ic \&Fa Ta a function argument
.It Ic \&Vt Ta a variable type
.It Ic \&Va Ta a variable name
.It Ic \&Dv Ta a defined constant
.It Ic \&Er Ta an error constant
.It Ic \&Rv Ta the standard return value text:
.Fl std Op Ar function ...
.El
.Ss Text and quotation
.Bl -column "Brq, Bro, Brc" description -compact
.It Ic \&Em Ta emphasis
.It Ic \&Sy Ta strong emphasis
.It Ic \&Li Ta literal text
.It Ic \&No Ta normal text inside another macro
.It Ic \&Ns Ta no space before the next word
.It Ic \&Ap Ta an apostrophe with no space around it
.It Ic \&Pf Ta a prefix with no space after it
.It Ic \&Dq , \&Do , \&Dc Ta double quotes
.It Ic \&Sq , \&So , \&Sc Ta single quotes
.It Ic \&Qq , \&Qo , \&Qc Ta typewriter double quotes
.It Ic \&Ql Ta quoted literal
.It Ic \&Pq , \&Po , \&Pc Ta parentheses
.It Ic \&Bq , \&Bo , \&Bc Ta brackets
.It Ic "Brq, Bro, Brc" Ta braces
.It Ic \&Aq , \&Ao , \&Ac Ta angle brackets
.It Ic \&Eo , \&Ec Ta enclosure with any characters
.It Ic \&Xo , \&Xc Ta extend a macro's arguments over lines
.El
.Ss References and authors
.Bl -column "Brq, Bro, Brc" description -compact
.It Ic \&Xr Ta a cross-reference:
.Ar name section
.It Ic \&Lk Ta a hyperlink:
.Ar url Op Ar text
.It Ic \&Mt Ta an e-mail address
.It Ic \&An Ta an author's name;
.Fl split ,
.Fl nosplit
.It Ic \&Rs , \&Re Ta a bibliographic reference, with the fields
.Ic \&%A
(author),
.Ic \&%B
(book),
.Ic \&%C
(city),
.Ic \&%D
(date),
.Ic \&%I
(issuer),
.Ic \&%J
(journal),
.Ic \&%N
(number),
.Ic \&%O
(other),
.Ic \&%P
(pages),
.Ic \&%Q
(institution),
.Ic \&%R
(report),
.Ic \&%T
(title),
.Ic \&%U
(URL) and
.Ic \&%V
(volume)
.It Ic \&St Ta a standard, such as
.Fl p1003.1-2008
.It Ic \&At , \&Bx , \&Bsx , \&Dx , \&Fx , \&Nx , \&Ox , \&Ux Ta
AT&T, BSD and other systems, with an optional version
.El
.Ss Controlling spacing
.Bl -column "Brq, Bro, Brc" description -compact
.It Ic \&Sm Ta spacing mode:
.Cm on
or
.Cm off
.El
.Sh MACRO SYNTAX
Macros belong to one of these classes:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Block, full-explicit
Enclose everything up to a closing macro:
.Ic \&Bd ,
.Ic \&Bf ,
.Ic \&Bk ,
.Ic \&Bl ,
.Ic \&Rs .
.It Block, full-implicit
Enclose everything up to the next macro of the same or a higher level:
.Ic \&Sh ,
.Ic \&Ss ,
.Ic \&It ,
.Ic \&Nd ,
and
.Ic \&Nm
at the start of a line in SYNOPSIS.
.It Block, partial-explicit
Enclose the rest of the line and later lines up to a closing macro, which
may itself be on the middle of a line:
.Ic \&Oo ,
.Ic \&Do ,
.Ic \&Xo
and the other opening macros above.
.It Block, partial-implicit
Enclose the rest of their line:
.Ic \&Op ,
.Ic \&Dq ,
.Ic \&Sq
and the other quoting macros, and
.Ic \&D1 ,
.Ic \&Dl .
.It In-line
Format their own arguments:
.Ic \&Fl ,
.Ic \&Ar ,
.Ic \&Pa
and most others.
.El
.Pp
Most macros are
.Em parsed :
an argument that is the name of a
.Em callable
macro starts that macro, so that
.Ql .Op Fl a Ar file
gives
.Op Fl a Ar file .
Prefix a word with
.Ql \e&
to use it literally.
.Sh DELIMITERS
The punctuation characters
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&, ,
.Ql \&: ,
.Ql \&; ,
.Ql \&) ,
.Ql \&] ,
.Ql \&?
and
.Ql \&! ,
given as separate arguments, close a macro: they are printed without a
space before them and outside its font.
.Ql \&(
and
.Ql \&[
open: they are printed before the macro, without a space after them.
.Ql \&|
separates, with spaces on both sides.
Written as
.Ql \e&.\&
they are ordinary words.
.Pp
A closing
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&?
or
.Ql \&!
at the end of a macro line ends a sentence, like one at the end of a text
line.
